%T A398350 5297,6067,6311,6367,9467,11719,12829,15149,15307,15901,17471,18329,
%U A398350 19477,20183,20341,20719,22067,23887,24091,25609,26687,26717,27767,30047,30097,32467,33107,33851
%N A398350 Primes p that start a run of three consecutive primes in arithmetic progression (CPAP-3) such that the two primes immediately preceding p, or the two primes immediately following the run, form a twin prime pair.
%C A398350 A CPAP-3 is a run of three consecutive primes p, q, r (with no other primes in between) such that q - p = r - q; see A047948 for the case of common difference 6 and A006560 for background.
%C A398350 This sequence selects those CPAP-3 whose start p is immediately preceded by a twin prime pair (A001359), or whose end r is immediately followed by one.
%C A398350 Of the 5433 CPAP-3 runs with p < 2*10^6, 973 (about 18%) have this property.
%C A398350 a(3) = 251 satisfies both conditions: the twin pair (239, 241) immediately precedes 251, and the twin pair (269, 271) immediately follows the run 251, 257, 263. (Note that 251, 257, 263, 269 is in fact a CPAP-4; the run 251, 257, 263 qualifies as a CPAP-3 start.)
%C A398350 The restriction to twin primes is structurally distinguished rather than arbitrary: a twin partner sits at the closest possible distance, where residue conditions are most restrictive. For CPAP-4 runs with d == 12 (mod 30) an adjacent twin is structurally impossible, since the starting residue mod 5 is forced to 2, making both neighboring positions divisible by 5; no comparable exclusion exists for arbitrary neighboring primes.
%e A398350 47 is a term: 47, 53, 59 are consecutive primes in arithmetic progression (common difference 6), and the two primes immediately preceding 47 are the twin prime pair (41, 43).
%e A398350 199 is a term: 199, 211, 223 are consecutive primes in arithmetic progression (common difference 12), and the two primes immediately following 223 are the twin prime pair (227, 229).
%p A398350 P:= select(isprime,[2,seq(i,i=3..10^5,2)]):
%p A398350 G:= P[2..-1]-P[1..-2]:
%p A398350 J:= select(i -> G[i] = G[i+1] and (G[i+3] = 2 or G[i-2] = 2), [$2 .. nops(G)-3]):
%p A398350 P[J]; # _Robert Israel_, Aug 05 2026
%o A398350 (Python)
%o A398350 from sympy import primerange
%o A398350 primes = list(primerange(40000))
%o A398350 terms = [3]  # edge case: run 3, 5, 7 is followed by the twin pair (11, 13)
%o A398350 for i in range(2, len(primes)-4):
%o A398350     p, q, r = primes[i], primes[i+1], primes[i+2]
%o A398350     if q - p != r - q:
%o A398350         continue
%o A398350     before = primes[i-1] - primes[i-2] == 2
%o A398350     after = primes[i+4] - primes[i+3] == 2
%o A398350     if before or after:
%o A398350         terms.append(p)
%o A398350 print(terms)
%Y A398350 Cf. A047948, A006560, A001359, A006562.
%K A398350 nonn,new
%O A398350 1,1
%A A398350 _Riccardo Mosch_, Jul 26 2026
